---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import Code from '../components/Code.astro'

const hostMode = `
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [ownci]
    container: ubuntu:24.04     # required in host mode
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: make test
`

const containerRunner = `
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [ownci]            # no container: needed
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: sudo apt-get install -y jq && make test
`

const team = `
jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: [ownci, "\${{ github.actor }}"]   # the runner of whoever pushed
    container: node:22
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: npm ci && npm run build
`
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How OwnCI works"
  description="GitHub keeps the workflows, the queue and the PR check. OwnCI runs the official GitHub Actions runner on your machine, puts every job in a container and wipes the workspace afterwards."
>
  <main id="main" class="content-page">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-dot"></span> How it works</p>
      <h1>GitHub runs the show.<br /><span>Your machine runs the job.</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">
        OwnCI doesn’t implement a CI protocol. It downloads, registers, supervises and cleans up
        the official GitHub Actions runner, and configures it so every job runs inside a Docker
        container.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="owns-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">Who owns what</p>
        <h2 id="owns-title">A clear split of responsibilities.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="compare">
        <div class="compare-col">
          <h3>GitHub keeps</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Your workflow files and when they trigger</li>
            <li>Queuing and scheduling jobs</li>
            <li>The check on the pull request</li>
            <li>Job logs in the Actions tab</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-col ours">
          <h3>OwnCI takes care of</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Downloading the pinned runner and verifying its checksum</li>
            <li>Registering it with an opt-in <code>ownci</code> label</li>
            <li>Supervising the process: start, stop, restart with backoff</li>
            <li>Enforcing container jobs and wiping the workspace after each</li>
            <li>Reporting what is actually true with <code>status</code> and <code>doctor</code></li>
            <li>Unregistering and removing everything with <code>uninstall</code></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="job-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">One job, start to finish</p>
        <h2 id="job-title">From push to green check.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="flow-step"><span>01</span><strong>You push</strong><p>A pull request triggers a workflow whose job says <code>runs-on: [ownci]</code>.</p></div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step"><span>02</span><strong>GitHub queues it</strong><p>The job waits for an online runner with the <code>ownci</code> label.</p></div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step owned"><span>03</span><strong>Your runner takes it</strong><p>The official runner, supervised by OwnCI, runs the job inside a container on your Docker.</p></div>
        <div class="flow-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step owned"><span>04</span><strong>Wiped for the next</strong><p>A hook wipes the workspace in a throwaway, network-less container. If that fails, the job fails visibly.</p></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="modes-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">Two ways to run</p>
        <h2 id="modes-title">Pick the mode that fits your workflows.</h2>
        <p>Both keep every job in a container. They differ in where the runner itself lives.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid two">
        <article class="card">
          <span class="card-number">HOST MODE · DEFAULT</span>
          <h3>Jobs declare their container</h3>
          <p>
            The runner runs on your machine, and every job must say <code>container:</code>. A job
            without one is rejected by the runner, so nothing runs directly on your host.
          </p>
          <Code label="workflow.yml" code={hostMode} />
        </article>
        <article class="card accent">
          <span class="card-number">CONTAINER-RUNNER MODE</span>
          <h3>Workflows run unchanged</h3>
          <p>
            The runner itself runs in a container, a fresh one for every job. Jobs without <code>container:</code> run inside it as your user, with <code>sudo</code>; jobs with
            one behave as in host mode.
          </p>
          <Code label="workflow.yml" code={containerRunner} />
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="callout">
        Switch with <code>ownci setup --repo owner/name --mode container-runner</code>. The default
        image is the official <code>ghcr.io/actions/actions-runner</code>, pinned by digest. <a href="/docs/workflows/">Details in the docs →</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="split-section" aria-labelledby="team-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="section-kicker">For teams</p>
        <h2 id="team-title">One workflow, everyone’s own machine.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Give each developer’s runner their GitHub username as an extra label, and let the
          workflow pick the runner of whoever triggered it. Your pushes run on your machine, your
          teammate’s on theirs.
        </p>
        <Code label="workflow.yml" code={team} />
        <p>
          If someone’s runner is offline, their job waits in the queue. It never lands on someone
          else’s machine.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="cta-band">
      <span class="cta-spark" aria-hidden="true">◉</span>
      <div><p class="section-kicker">Next</p><h2>Know exactly what a container does and doesn’t protect.</h2><p>Read the trust model before you set up your first runner.</p></div>
      <a class="button-primary" href="/security/">Security <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
